      "text": "To find the area of the square and the area of the circle, we first need to determine the side length of the square.\n\nGiven that the diagonal of the square is 5 inches, we can use the Pythagorean theorem to find the side length. Let \\( s \\) be the side length of the square. Then, we have:\n\n\\[ s\\sqrt{2} = 5 \\]\n\nSolving for \\( s \\):\n\n\\[ s = \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\]\n\nThe area of the square is:\n\n\\[ \\text{Area}_{\\text{square}} = s^2 = \\left( \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right)^2 = \\frac{25 \\cdot 2}{4} = \\frac{50}{4} = 12.5 \\text{ square inches} \\]\n\nNext, we find the radius of the circle. Given that the diameter of the circle is 5 inches, the radius is:\n\n\\[ r = \\frac{5}{2} = 2.5 \\text{ inches} \\]\n\nThe area of the circle is:\n\n\\[ \\text{Area}_{\\text{circle}} = \\pi r^2 = \\pi (2.5)^2 = \\pi \\cdot 6.25 \\approx 19.635 \\text{ square inches} \\]\n\nTo find out by how many square inches the area of the circle is greater than the area of the square, we subtract the area of the square from the area of the circle:\n\n\\[ \\text{Difference} = \\text{Area}_{\\text{circle}} - \\text{Area}_{\\text{square}} \\approx 19.635 - 12.5 = 7.135 \\]\n\nRounding to the nearest tenth, the difference is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{7.1} \\]",
