      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine by how many square inches the area of the circle is greater than the area of the square, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the side length of the square.\n2. Calculate the area of the square.\n3. Calculate the area of the circle.\n4. Find the difference between the area of the circle and the area of the square.\n\n**Step 1: Calculate the side length of the square.**\n\nThe diagonal of the square is given as 5 inches. If the side length of the square is \\( s \\), then the diagonal can be found using the Pythagorean theorem:\n\\[ s\\sqrt{2} = 5 \\]\nSolving for \\( s \\):\n\\[ s = \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\]\n\n**Step 2: Calculate the area of the square.**\n\nThe area \\( A_{\\text{square}} \\) of the square is:\n\\[ A_{\\text{square}} = s^2 = \\left( \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right)^2 = \\frac{25 \\cdot 2}{4} = \\frac{50}{4} = 12.5 \\]\n\n**Step 3: Calculate the area of the circle.**\n\nThe diameter of the circle is 5 inches, so the radius \\( r \\) is:\n\\[ r = \\frac{5}{2} = 2.5 \\]\nThe area \\( A_{\\text{circle}} \\) of the circle is:\n\\[ A_{\\text{circle}} = \\pi r^2 = \\pi (2.5)^2 = \\pi \\cdot 6.25 = 6.25\\pi \\]\n\n**Step 4: Find the difference between the area of the circle and the area of the square.**\n\nThe difference in area is:\n\\[ A_{\\text{circle}} - A_{\\text{square}} = 6.25\\pi - 12.5 \\]\nUsing the approximation \\(\\pi \\approx 3.14159\\):\n\\[ 6.25\\pi \\approx 6.25 \\times 3.14159 = 19.6349375 \\]\n\\[ 6.25\\pi - 12.5 \\approx 19.6349375 - 12.5 = 7.1349375 \\]\n\nRounding to the nearest tenth:\n\\[ 7.1349375 \\approx 7.1 \\]\n\nTherefore, the area of the circle is greater than the area of the square by \\(\\boxed{7.1}\\) square inches.",
