      "text": "First, we need to find the side length of the square using the given diagonal. Let's denote the side length of the square as $s$. The relationship between the side length and the diagonal of a square can be described by the equation:\n\\[ s\\sqrt{2} = 5. \\]\nSolving for $s$, we get:\n\\[ s = \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}. \\]\nThe area of the square is then:\n\\[ \\text{Area}_{\\text{square}} = s^2 = \\left( \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right)^2 = \\frac{25 \\cdot 2}{4} = \\frac{50}{4} = 12.5. \\]\nNext, we need to find the area of the circle. The diameter of the circle is given as 5 inches, so the radius is:\n\\[ r = \\frac{5}{2} = 2.5. \\]\nThe area of the circle is:\n\\[ \\text{Area}_{\\text{circle}} = \\pi r^2 = \\pi (2.5)^2 = \\pi \\cdot 6.25 = 6.25\\pi. \\]\nUsing the approximation $\\pi \\approx 3.14159$, we get:\n\\[ \\text{Area}_{\\text{circle}} \\approx 6.25 \\cdot 3.14159 = 19.635. \\]\nTo find the difference in area, we subtract the area of the square from the area of the circle:\n\\[ \\text{Area}_{\\text{circle}} - \\text{Area}_{\\text{square}} \\approx 19.635 - 12.5 = 7.135. \\]\nRounded to the nearest tenth, the difference is:\n\\[ \\boxed{7.1}. \\]",
